1
00:00:00,295 --> 00:00:02,635
I just tore a chunk off this QR code.

2
00:00:02,645 --> 00:00:04,195
It still scans.

3
00:00:04,389 --> 00:00:07,489
That's because most of it
isn't your message at all.

4
00:00:07,975 --> 00:00:09,065
So what's the rest?

5
00:00:09,075 --> 00:00:10,650
A backup copy?

6
00:00:10,798 --> 00:00:13,973
Smudge one letter, and
the two copies disagree.

7
00:00:14,056 --> 00:00:15,071
Which one's right?

8
00:00:15,081 --> 00:00:16,731
Copies can't tell you.

9
00:00:16,993 --> 00:00:20,968
Reed-Solomon codes, from
1960, do something smarter.

10
00:00:21,444 --> 00:00:23,694
Say your message is just two numbers.

11
00:00:23,780 --> 00:00:26,330
Two dots fix exactly one line.

12
00:00:26,695 --> 00:00:29,345
Now add extra dots on that same line.

13
00:00:29,510 --> 00:00:33,274
Tear any two away: the survivors
still fix the line, and it puts

14
00:00:33,284 --> 00:00:34,984
the missing dots back.

15
00:00:35,420 --> 00:00:37,470
But a scanner can't see holes.

16
00:00:37,528 --> 00:00:40,554
To a scanner, a tear
looks like a wrong number.

17
00:00:40,793 --> 00:00:42,253
So which dot is lying?

18
00:00:42,263 --> 00:00:43,938
The one that's off the line.

19
00:00:44,137 --> 00:00:48,687
Every liar costs two extra dots:
one finds it, one fixes it.

20
00:00:49,145 --> 00:00:51,970
A QR code does the same trick, with bytes.

21
00:00:52,064 --> 00:00:53,949
This one has twenty-six bytes.

22
00:00:53,959 --> 00:00:55,269
Nine are your message.

23
00:00:55,279 --> 00:00:57,404
Seventeen are extra points.

24
00:00:57,591 --> 00:00:59,856
At two per liar, it can fix any eight.

25
00:00:59,866 --> 00:01:01,515
About thirty percent.

26
00:01:01,756 --> 00:01:03,796
That's why a logo can sit in the middle.

27
00:01:03,806 --> 00:01:05,931
It's damage, on purpose.

28
00:01:06,382 --> 00:01:10,107
The catch: that's thirty percent
of the bytes, not of the paper.

29
00:01:10,255 --> 00:01:13,070
These three corner squares
aren't protected at all.

30
00:01:13,080 --> 00:01:15,355
They're how the scanner finds the code.

31
00:01:15,466 --> 00:01:18,166
Tear into one, and this code won't scan.

32
00:01:18,612 --> 00:01:19,752
That first tear?

33
00:01:19,762 --> 00:01:22,187
It took six of the nine message bytes.

34
00:01:22,278 --> 00:01:24,828
The extra points rebuilt every one.

35
00:01:25,090 --> 00:01:27,405
A torn QR code isn't lucky.

36
00:01:27,415 --> 00:01:29,665
It was built expecting to be torn.
